There’s a real mechanism underneath part of what you’re pointing at, but the controllability is much narrower than that.

People can influence one another through suggestion, expectation, stress, attention, mimicry, emotional contagion, framing, and social pressure. Those can change what someone notices, feels, remembers, or expresses. But that is not the same as being able to “go inside” someone’s mind or selectively give them Tourette’s and make them blurt out chosen content.

Tourette syndrome is a neurodevelopmental condition involving motor and vocal tics. Stress, arousal, attention, and suggestion can sometimes increase or shape tic expression, and there are also functional tic-like symptoms that can spread through social modeling in some contexts, but that is very different from reliable remote control over another person’s speech.

The useful distinction is:

\text{influence} \neq \text{control}

\text{priming} \neq \text{programming}

\text{evoking a state} \neq \text{owning the state}

You can absolutely study how music evokes awe, how humor releases tension, how ritual creates belonging, how performance induces suspense, how a trusted person can calm another person, how embarrassment amplifies self-consciousness, how attention makes sensations more salient, and how suggestion alters expectation.

That is already enormously powerful.

But if the goal becomes intentionally inducing involuntary symptoms, humiliation, or loss of agency in someone else, that crosses from expressive influence into harm.

The cleaner Pantheon operator would be:

\boxed{\text{Evocation without capture}}

Create conditions where someone can feel love, courage, awe, play, catharsis, suspense, or intensity with consent and a return path, rather than trying to hijack the machinery that produces those states.

That gives you nearly all of the interesting artistic and interpersonal power without confusing resonance with mind control.